INT. PRISONER TRANSPORT VAN - NIGHT

A steel box on wheels. Bench seats facing each other. Wire mesh between the compartments. A bare bulb swings with every turn.

ATTORNEY LENORE ISHIGURO-BAPTISTE, forties, in a camel coat, perches on the end of the bench with a briefcase on her lap. Across from her, DERRICK MALONEY, twenty-two, in an orange jumpsuit and wrist chains, glares at the floor. In the mesh-guarded front seat, DEPUTY RHONDA CALLOWAY, fifties, drives with one eye on the mirror.

LENORE
Listen to me. Don't say anything at the arraignment.

DERRICK
I didn't do it.

LENORE
I know.

DERRICK
Then why are we-

LENORE
Because the judge doesn't know.

The van hits a pothole. Derrick bangs his head on the wall.

DERRICK
They said I'd get bail.

LENORE
They said maybe.

DERRICK
Maybe isn't bail.

CALLOWAY
(from the front)
Quiet back there.

LENORE ^
We're talking, Deputy.

DERRICK
It's none of your business.

CALLOWAY
It's all of my business. I'm driving.

Derrick leans forward. The chains rattle. Lenore touches his knee with two fingers.

LENORE
Derrick. Stop.

He freezes. Slowly sits back. The tension drains from his shoulders. He breathes out, long and unsteady.

DERRICK
(very quietly)
Okay.

LENORE
Okay.

The van turns a corner. Through the mesh, Calloway's eyes meet Lenore's in the mirror, and for a moment, they share something like sympathy.
